---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '@/components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import CatalogueList from '@/components/CatalogueList.astro';

import config from '@/config';

const { title, description } = config.pages.components;
const all = (await getCollection('catalogue')).sort((a, b) => a.data.name.localeCompare(b.data.name));

// Test suites and harnesses live on the Testing page, where the sequence they belong to is told.
const entries = all.filter((entry) => entry.data.tier !== 'tooling');

const standards = [...new Set(entries.flatMap((entry) => entry.data.standards))].sort();

// A composition reference that points nowhere is a broken catalogue, so fail the build rather than
// render a dangling name. The per-entry schema cannot see across entries, so the check lives here.
const ids = new Set(all.map((entry) => entry.id));
for (const entry of all) {
  for (const ref of entry.data.composedOf) {
    if (!ids.has(ref)) {
      throw new Error(`catalogue: "${entry.id}" is composed of "${ref}", which is not an entry.`);
    }
  }
}

const proposed = entries.filter((entry) => entry.data.maturity === 'proposed');
const existing = entries.length - proposed.length;
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <Hero title={title} description={description} />

  <Section>
    <p class="lead">
      Components live across four homes, so an entry below often points somewhere other than this
      organisation. Each one records where the component is today, not where it is expected to end
      up. A pending move or donation says so, without a date nobody has agreed.
    </p>

    <p class="counts">
      <strong>{existing}</strong> components exist. <strong>{proposed.length}</strong> are proposed
      and do not exist: no repository, no code, nobody committed to building them. A proposed entry
      has a dashed outline and names neither a home nor a maintainer, because it has neither. The
      whole set is here so that the architecture can be read and argued with. It is not a roadmap and
      not a schedule.
    </p>
    {
      standards.length > 0 && (
        <p class="standards">
          <span>Standards served</span>
          {standards.join(' · ')}
        </p>
      )
    }
    <p class="add">
      Test suites, reference implementations and the harnesses an implementer runs against are on the
      <a href="/testing">Testing</a> page, with the sequence they belong to.
    </p>

    <p class="add">
      Add a component by opening a pull request against <code>content/catalogue.yaml</code> in
      <a href="https://github.com/trade-commons/trade-commons.github.io" rel="noreferrer">
        this site's repository
      </a>. The entry is where somebody states who is accountable when it breaks, which is why the
      schema requires a maintainer.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section variant="gray">
    <CatalogueList entries={entries} />
  </Section>
</Layout>

<style lang="scss">
  .lead {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
  }

  .standards {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    padding: var(--spacing-sm);
    background: var(--color-gray-50);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-gray-200);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);

    span {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 0.3em;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--color-gray-500);
      font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
      font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    }
  }

  .add {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--color-gray-700);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  .counts {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    padding-left: 1em;
    color: var(--color-gray-700);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-gray-300);
  }
</style>
